cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "tallymap: divergent Monte Carlo loops as a JAX primitive"
message: "If you use this software, please cite it using these metadata."
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Omar
    family-names: Alterkait
    email: omar.alterkait@tufts.edu
version: 0.6.0rc1
license: Apache-2.0
repository-code: "https://github.com/OmarAlterkait/tallymap"
url: "https://github.com/OmarAlterkait/tallymap"
abstract: >-
  tallymap maps a function over many independent Monte Carlo items, each running its own data-dependent loop,
  and sums their results into commutative tallies. It is one JAX primitive, so jit, vmap, grad, jvp, jacfwd,
  jacrev, hessian and shard_map all compose with it, and a call means exactly jax.vmap of the item followed by a
  sum. On an NVIDIA GPU the item compiles to a single CUDA kernel in which each thread runs one item at that item's
  own trip count and then claims the next; on every other platform it runs a masked plain-JAX interpreter. Reverse mode replays each item from its inputs, so gradient memory does not grow with the trip
  count, which is something plain JAX cannot do for a while loop at all.
keywords:
  - JAX
  - Monte Carlo
  - automatic differentiation
  - GPU
  - CUDA
